% ===== Abstract =====
\begin{abstract}
We investigate a resource-centric view of predictive process monitoring (PPM) that aims to capture shared-resource competition and concurrency, a known blind spot of prevalent case-centric sequence models. We propose to learn simple per-resource multinomial logistic decision policies from local XES logs and to couple them with a lightweight discrete-event simulator to roll out futures from any running prefix. Aggregating 10--30 Monte Carlo rollouts would yield global next-event and per-case next-activity/suffix predictions as well as resource-level workload forecasts. In this paper we detail the design, discuss the anticipated benefits and risks, and release a reproducible baseline built on a minimal one-layer LSTM trained on case sequences. On three public logs (BPI 2012, BPI 2017, Road Traffic Fine Management) loaded via PM4Py, the LSTM attains strong next-activity accuracy (Top-1 up to 0.83, Top-3 above 0.99) with short training. We highlight practical pitfalls encountered during data preparation and plotting, and we position the proposed resource-centric simulator as a complementary pathway to bring concurrency-aware predictions and resource workload forecasts to PPM.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}
Predictive process monitoring (PPM) produces forecasts about ongoing cases, such as next activity, remaining time, or outcome \cite{ceravolo2024predictivepm,aalikhani2024comparativeao}. Most operational processes are concurrent and share resources across cases, yet mainstream PPM has been predominantly case-centric and sequence-based, typically ignoring contention for shared resources. This leads to blind spots in predictions that depend on resource availability or workload patterns. Object-centric and resource-aware lines of work have emphasized the need to model entities beyond the single case notion \cite{rohrer2022predictiveop,smit2024hoegan}, and simulation has long been advocated in BPM to capture concurrency \cite{vuksic2017utilizationod,bocciarelli2024simulationbasedpp}. Still, a light-weight, data-driven approach that plugs resource behavior into PPM predictions remains underexplored.

We propose a resource-centric PPM agent that learns per-resource decision policies from local XES logs and combines them with a small discrete-event simulator to roll out multiple futures from any process prefix. The approach aims to complement case-centric sequence models with explicit competition for shared resources, providing both case-level predictions (next activity, suffix, remaining time) and resource-level forecasts (next tasks, workloads). Our short hypothesis is that such per-resource policies plus a light simulator can improve next-event and suffix predictions while adding actionable resource forecasts.

This paper contributes: (i) a concise formulation of resource-centric PPM via per-resource multinomial logistic policies and a discrete-event simulator; (ii) a reproducible case-centric LSTM baseline with competitive accuracy across three public logs; (iii) a critical discussion of engineering pitfalls we faced, including timestamp indexing and missing prefix-length plots; and (iv) a roadmap for experiments that will directly test whether concurrency-aware rollouts improve predictions over the case-centric baseline.

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}
Sequence models have been widely used for PPM tasks such as next activity and remaining time prediction \cite{aalikhani2024comparativeao,yarlagadda2024predictivemi}, often leveraging LSTMs \cite{hochreiter1997longsm,goodfellow2016deep} or attention over traces and graphs \cite{deng2024enhancingpp}. While effective, these models are case-centric and typically abstract away resource contention.

Object-centric and resource-centric perspectives address this limitation by representing multiple interacting entities and their relations \cite{rohrer2022predictiveop,smit2024hoegan}. Such representations aim to capture concurrency and shared-resource effects that are otherwise lost in case-centric logs. In a complementary direction, discrete-event simulation (DES) and hybrid approaches have a long history in BPM for reproducing concurrency and exploring what-if scenarios \cite{vuksic2017utilizationod,bocciarelli2024simulationbasedpp}. Recent work also connects predictive monitoring to resource allocation optimization \cite{park2023optimizingra}.

Our work draws from both lines: we retain the simplicity of learning local policies from standard XES event logs processed with PM4Py \cite{berti2023pm4pyap}, while introducing a compact DES to emulate competition for shared resources. The policies are intentionally simple---multinomial logistic regression \cite{madahana2024predictivemo}---to keep attribution clear and latency low, and we use Monte Carlo rollouts to aggregate predictions across potential futures \cite{zhang2023optimizationof}. In contrast to object-centric deep architectures \cite{smit2024hoegan}, our simulator explicitly enforces resource availability constraints, allowing us to derive resource-level workload forecasts alongside case-level predictions. The present study focuses on establishing a strong and transparent baseline and documenting pitfalls, with a forthcoming evaluation of the resource-centric variant.

\section{Background}
\label{sec:background}
We adopt standard terminology: an event log is a multiset of traces, each trace belonging to a case and comprising timestamped events with attributes such as activity, lifecycle, and resource. We target next-activity, next-event, suffix, remaining-time, and resource workload predictions. Logs are parsed from XES into tabular form using PM4Py \cite{berti2023pm4pyap}. For the baseline, we follow the common practice of retaining completed events and constructing chronological prefixes per case \cite{aalikhani2024comparativeao}. For the simulator, we consider a classical DES loop that maintains queues of waiting cases per activity, resource busy/idle states, and event completion times \cite{vuksic2017utilizationod}.

\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}
The proposed resource-centric agent learns per-resource decision policies from local logs and predicts process futures via Monte Carlo simulation. From any observed prefix, the simulator rolls out N independent trajectories, producing distributions over next events (global and per case), suffixes, remaining time, and resource workloads. While we defer full empirical evaluation of this agent to future work, we formalize its components to enable reproducible implementation.

Data preparation and state. We process logs to ensure lifecycle transitions are either paired start/complete with durations or treated as instantaneous complete events when starts are unavailable. Cases are split chronologically by case start into train/validation/test. For simulation, the state includes: the next required activity per running case based on control-flow replay, the busy/idle status of each resource with expected completion times, and queues of waiting cases per activity with counts and waiting times.

Per-resource policies. For each resource r we fit a multinomial logistic regression p(a | context) to select the next activity when r becomes free. The context includes the previous activity handled by r, a four-bin time-of-day encoding, and queue descriptors such as activity-wise counts and age of the oldest waiting case. To avoid overfitting for underrepresented resources, we back off to a global policy trained across all resources. We choose logistic regression for transparency, efficiency, and robustness \cite{madahana2024predictivemo}.

Activity durations. For each activity we fit a log-normal distribution of service times from paired start/complete observations; in sparse regimes, we fall back to the median duration. Calendar effects can be incorporated via mixture components but are omitted here for simplicity.

Discrete-event simulator. The simulator maintains a priority queue of next completion events. When a completion occurs, the resource r that just freed samples an activity via its policy. Among cases requiring that activity, we assign the oldest waiting case (FIFO). We sample the activity's duration and schedule its completion. This loop continues until a specified horizon (e.g., the next event for each active case, or a bounded suffix length).

Monte Carlo rollouts and aggregation. From any prefix, we run N rollouts (we target N=30 for a latency-accuracy trade-off). We aggregate: (i) per-case next-activity distributions and suffixes by majority vote or probability averaging; (ii) global next-event predictions by taking the earliest simulated completion across resources; and (iii) resource-level forecasts by counting scheduled assignments and estimating short-horizon workloads. Rollout-based estimates trade reduced bias for increased variance; we keep N small to control latency \cite{zhang2023optimizationof}.

Baseline LSTM. To anchor results, we implement a one-layer LSTM that predicts the next activity from the sequence of past activities and simple temporal features (delta time, time since case start, hour, weekday, working-hour flag). The LSTM uses an embedding for activities and concatenates continuous features at each step before the recurrent layer. We train for ten epochs with Adam, selecting the checkpoint with the best validation Top-3 accuracy and reporting test metrics \cite{hochreiter1997longsm,goodfellow2016deep}.

\section{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:experimental_setup}
Datasets. We use three public logs: BPI Challenge 2012 \cite{bose2012processma}, BPI Challenge 2017 \cite{jeong2017bpic2}, and the Road Traffic Fine Management Process \cite{jlidi2024navigatingpm}. XES logs are loaded via PM4Py \cite{berti2023pm4pyap} from a local input directory using a robust discovery utility. We restrict to completed events and sort by timestamp.

Splitting and prefixes. We perform a chronological 70/15/15 split by case start time. Prefixes of length 1 to 10 are constructed per case, ensuring targets exist by stopping at T-1 for a trace of length T. We standardize the first two continuous features (delta time and time since start) using training statistics.

Baseline training. The LSTM has a 64-dimensional activity embedding, five continuous features per step, a single recurrent layer with 128 hidden units, dropout 0.2, and a linear classifier over the activity vocabulary. We train with cross-entropy, batch size 128, learning rate 1e-3, and early selection on validation Top-3.

Metrics. We report cross-entropy loss, Top-1 and Top-3 next-activity accuracy, and macro F1 on the next-activity classification task. For this baseline run, we did not compute suffix similarity, remaining-time MAE, or resource-level metrics; these will be enabled by the simulator in future experiments.

Engineering pitfalls. We encountered a crash due to integer indexing into a pandas Series with a non-RangeIndex when building prefixes; converting timestamps to dense numpy arrays assured positional indexing and resolved off-by-one risks. Additionally, some plotting utilities expected prefix-length diagnostics that were not recorded by the baseline, resulting in empty prefix-length distribution figures. We preserved only consistent plots for the write-up.

\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}
We trained the LSTM baseline on each dataset. Validation Top-3 accuracy increased rapidly and stabilized, and training/validation losses converged closely, with mild overfitting observed on BPI2012. Representative curves are shown in Figure~\ref{fig:loss_curves}. Confusion matrices for BPI2012 and ROAD show strong diagonals with limited confusions (Figure~\ref{fig:cms}).

Quantitatively, the final test metrics for next-activity prediction were as follows. On BPI2012: loss 0.5355, Top-1 0.7569, macro F1 0.5872, Top-3 0.9874. On BPI2017: loss 0.3877, Top-1 0.8332, macro F1 0.5710, Top-3 0.9906. On ROAD: loss 0.4833, Top-1 0.8020, macro F1 0.4740, Top-3 0.9936. These figures confirm that even a minimal case-centric model can achieve high Top-3 accuracy across diverse logs with limited epochs.

These results do not yet reflect any concurrency-aware modeling. They establish a transparent baseline against which we will test the resource-centric agent. In particular, we will assess whether per-resource policies and DES rollouts improve next-event and suffix predictions and whether they enable accurate resource-level forecasts such as per-resource next-task precision and workload MAPE. We will also include an ablation that replaces learned policies with a FIFO-only rule inside the same simulator to isolate the value of learned resource behavior.

