\subsection*{Discussion of pitfalls and lessons}
The baseline surfaced two practical pitfalls that are common in PPM pipelines. First, time handling at prefix generation is fragile: mixing pandas Series with positional indexing can cause silent errors and crashes. Explicit conversion to numpy arrays and carefully bounding prefix lengths avoided off-by-one mistakes and ensured target existence. Second, plotting diagnostics require that the training script persist the needed artifacts. Our prefix-length distribution and Top-3 versus prefix-length plots were empty because we did not store the per-sample prefix length and Top-3 flags. We thus retained only validated plots for the figures and updated our logging protocol to capture the missing diagnostics going forward. These lessons are especially relevant when integrating a simulator, where alignment between replay state, prefix definition, and rollout horizon is critical to avoid leakage or inconsistent metrics.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}
We presented a resource-centric perspective for PPM that learns simple per-resource decision policies and uses a lightweight discrete-event simulator to produce concurrency-aware predictions and resource-level forecasts. We described the design and risks, and we established a transparent LSTM baseline with strong Top-3 accuracy across three public logs. The engineering pitfalls we documented---notably timestamp indexing and incomplete logging of diagnostics---are easy to overlook but materially affect reproducibility and analysis in PPM.

Next, we will complete the implementation and evaluation of the resource-centric agent, including Monte Carlo rollouts, remaining-time estimation, and per-resource workload forecasting. We will compare against the case-centric baseline on next-event accuracy, suffix similarity, remaining-time MAE, and resource-level metrics, and we will include a FIFO-only ablation inside the same simulator to quantify the value of learned policies. We expect these results to help the community assess when simple, local resource policies plus DES rollouts can complement case-centric deep models for concurrent business processes, and when richer object-centric representations or more expressive policies are warranted \cite{rohrer2022predictiveop,smit2024hoegan,bocciarelli2024simulationbasedpp}.

\bibliography{references}
\bibliographystyle{agents4science_2025}

\appendix

\section*{\LARGE Supplementary Material}
\label{sec:appendix}

\section{Additional implementation notes}
We load XES files from a local input directory using a discovery routine that searches common paths and robustly handles \texttt{.xes.gz}. Event logs are standardized to the schema \{case\_id, activity, lifecycle, timestamp, resource\}. Timestamps are converted to UTC and sorted; we drop events with missing time. For the baseline, we retain complete transitions. For the simulator, if start/complete pairs exist we compute service times; otherwise, we treat completes as instantaneous.

Prefix construction enforces that a target exists by generating prefixes up to $T{-}1$ for a trace of length $T$. We compute per-step continuous features: delta time, time since case start, hour of day, weekday, and a working-hours flag. We standardize only the first two using training-set statistics.

The LSTM baseline uses a single recurrent layer with hidden size 128, a 64-dimensional activity embedding, and dropout 0.2 before the classifier. We train with Adam at 1e-3, batch size 128, for ten epochs and select the checkpoint with best validation Top-3 accuracy. We report test loss, Top-1, macro F1, and Top-3.

\section{Planned simulator evaluation}
We will implement per-resource multinomial logistic policies with L2 regularization and class balancing, back off to a global policy when resource-specific data are sparse, fit log-normal activity durations with a median fallback, and run $N{=}30$ rollouts per prefix. We will report next-activity Top-1/Top-3, suffix normalized Damerau--Levenshtein similarity, remaining-time MAE, global next-event accuracy, per-resource next-task precision, and workload MAPE. A FIFO-only ablation inside the same simulator will isolate the effect of learned policies. Findings will be contrasted with object-centric and simulation-based PPM literature \cite{rohrer2022predictiveop,smit2024hoegan,bocciarelli2024simulationbasedpp}.

\end{document}
