% ===== Abstract =====
\begin{abstract}
We study a resource-centric approach to predictive process monitoring (PPM) aimed at capturing concurrent execution and competition for shared resources. The method learns simple multinomial logistic decision policies per resource from local XES logs and embeds them in a lightweight discrete-event simulator to roll out futures from any running prefix. Monte Carlo aggregation over 10--30 rollouts produces next-activity and suffix predictions at the case level, global next-event forecasts, and resource-level workload projections. We implement end-to-end data loading via PM4Py and a minimal case-centric LSTM as a baseline. On BPI 2012, BPI 2017, and Road Traffic Fine Management logs, the baseline achieves strong top-3 next-activity accuracy and competitive top-1 accuracy. At submission time, we report the baseline results and an ablation-free simulator design with identified risks and evaluation protocol. This paper contributes a reproducible resource-centric blueprint, a careful discussion of pitfalls when integrating simulation with learning, and a transparent baseline to enable future head-to-head comparison.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}
Predictive process monitoring (PPM) aims to forecast forthcoming activities, remaining time, and outcomes of running cases by exploiting event logs \cite{ceravolo2024predictivepm,aalikhani2024comparativeao}. Typical case-centric models operate on a single-case perspective and abstract away resource contention and concurrency. In many settings, however, cases compete for shared resources and the dynamics of queues, schedules, and idle times strongly influence what happens next \cite{park2023optimizingra}. Ignoring these effects may limit predictive fidelity and the operational relevance of forecasts.

We propose a resource-centric PPM blueprint that decomposes decision making by resource: each resource learns a lightweight multinomial logistic policy that maps local context to a next-activity choice, while activity durations are modeled from the log. These ingredients drive a small discrete-event simulator (DES) to roll out concurrent futures. Compared with case-centric sequence models, this design exposes resource-level forecasts such as workload and utilization, and it can represent competition for capacity explicitly \cite{vuksic2017utilizationod,bocciarelli2024simulationbasedpp}.

This paper makes three contributions. First, we motivate and specify a minimal resource-centric simulator that integrates per-resource policies and duration models, with Monte Carlo rollouts to generate predictive distributions. Second, we provide a strong, transparent case-centric LSTM baseline \cite{hochreiter1997longsm,goodfellow2016deep} trained on prefixes extracted from PM4Py-parsed XES logs \cite{berti2023pm4pyap}. Third, we report baseline results on BPI 2012, BPI 2017, and Road Traffic logs and release figure assets and evaluation code. Our current results do not yet include simulator-based improvements; instead, we document how we will evaluate them and discuss expected benefits and risks in practical deployments.

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}
PPM has progressed from early sequence classification and regression to advanced deep learning and graph-based encodings \cite{aalikhani2024comparativeao,deng2024enhancingpp,yarlagadda2024predictivemi}. While case-centric models are effective for next-activity and remaining-time prediction, they typically do not represent shared resource contention or concurrency explicitly. Object-centric monitoring and heterogeneous event graphs have been proposed to capture richer relations among events and objects \cite{rohrer2022predictiveop,smit2024hoegan}, but their operational integration with simulation for resource reasoning remains an open opportunity.

Discrete-event simulation is a mature tool for analyzing operational systems under concurrency and constraints \cite{vuksic2017utilizationod,bocciarelli2024simulationbasedpp}. Integrating DES with learned policies is appealing for PPM: it can produce both case-centric predictions and resource-level indicators such as queue length and utilization. Yet, calibration, drift over long horizons, and unobserved priorities are known pitfalls \cite{srisurin2022evaluatingtl}.

From an operations standpoint, predictive monitoring can support resource allocation and scheduling decisions \cite{park2023optimizingra}. Our per-resource multinomial logistic policies provide a simple, interpretable decision model akin to local dispatching rules, while Monte Carlo simulation approximates the distribution over futures \cite{zhang2023optimizationof}. In contrast, our baseline LSTM adheres to the widely used case-centric formulation \cite{hochreiter1997longsm}, enabling clear head-to-head comparisons once the simulator is integrated.

\section{Background}
\label{sec:background}
We assume event logs in XES with at least case identifier, activity name, timestamp, resource, and possibly lifecycle transition. Tasks may have start and complete, enabling duration estimation. In PPM, we consider: next-activity prediction for an ongoing case prefix; suffix prediction; remaining time; and global next-event prediction (which case and activity completes next). Resource-centric questions include which activity a resource will pick when idle, expected workload per resource, and queueing delays.

A DES represents system state as a set of running tasks with completion times and queues of waiting cases per activity; events include task completion, resource freeing, and task start. A decision policy maps state to which activity a resource starts next. In our blueprint, policies are learned from the log per resource; durations are sampled from per-activity models; and Monte Carlo rollouts generate predictive samples of futures from any prefix.

\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}
We outline the resource-centric simulator and the case-centric LSTM baseline used for comparison.

Resource-centric simulator. For each resource $r$, we fit a multinomial logistic regression policy $\pi_r(a \mid s)$ that selects the next activity $a$ given local state features $s$. Features include previous activity identifier observed for the same resource, time-of-day (coarse bins), and simple queue descriptors such as per-activity queue counts and oldest waiting time. The policy is:
\[
\pi_r(a \mid s) = \frac{\exp(w_{r,a}^\top s + b_{r,a})}{\sum_{a'} \exp(w_{r,a'}^\top s + b_{r,a'})}.
\]
When data for a resource are sparse, we back off to a global policy $\pi_{\mathrm{global}}$. Activity durations are modeled via log-normal fits per activity; when insufficient data are available, we revert to the median duration. The simulator maintains for each activity the queue of waiting cases (FIFO) and the set of running tasks with end times. When a completion occurs and a resource becomes idle, we sample the resource's next activity via $\pi_r$, pick the next eligible case from the corresponding queue (FIFO), sample a duration, and schedule the completion. From any observed prefix state, we perform $N$ Monte Carlo rollouts (default $N=30$) to aggregate predictions: case-level next-activity and suffix, remaining time from completion times, global next event, and resource-level workload (e.g., expected queued items and busy time over a horizon).

Baseline. Our baseline is a minimal one-layer LSTM over case prefixes \cite{hochreiter1997longsm,goodfellow2016deep}. We use a token embedding for activities and concatenate simple continuous features (time delta, time since case start, hour of day, day of week, working-hour flag). The last hidden state feeds a linear layer for next-activity classification. We train with cross-entropy and evaluate top-1 and top-3 accuracy and macro-F1 on next-activity prediction. This baseline is deliberately simple to provide a conservative point of comparison for the forthcoming simulator.

\section{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:experimental_setup}
Data and loading. We use a robust loader that discovers local XES files under input/ or data/ and parses them with PM4Py \cite{berti2023pm4pyap}. We consider any of BPI 2012 \cite{bose2012processma}, BPI 2017 \cite{jeong2017bpic2}, and Road Traffic Fine Management \cite{jlidi2024navigatingpm}, loading whichever are available. We keep the columns case\_id, activity, lifecycle, timestamp, resource, and sort events by time then case.

Preprocessing. If lifecycle is present, we retain complete transitions for next-activity modeling; when start and complete exist, pairs will later support duration fitting for the simulator. If only complete is available, we treat activity duration as instantaneous for the baseline. We generate case prefixes of maximum length 10 and compute normalized time delta and time since start features using training statistics.

Splitting. We perform a chronological split by case start time into 70 percent training, 15 percent validation, and 15 percent test. To bound runtime, we cap to the earliest 5000 cases per dataset if present.

Model and training. The LSTM uses an activity embedding of size 64, concatenates 5 continuous features, and has a hidden size of 128 with dropout 0.2. We train for 10 epochs with Adam (learning rate 1e-3), selecting the checkpoint with best validation top-3 accuracy. We compute cross-entropy loss, top-1 accuracy, macro-F1, and top-3 accuracy on the test split. Confusion matrices are saved for qualitative inspection.

Evaluation plan for the simulator. Once integrated, we will compare against the LSTM on: case-level next-activity top-1/top-3; suffix similarity (normalized Damerau--Levenshtein), remaining-time MAE; global next-event accuracy; and resource-level forecasts, including per-resource next-task precision and workload MAPE. We will also run an ablation that replaces learned policies with FIFO-only selection while keeping the same simulator.

\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}
We report the case-centric LSTM baseline on the three datasets. Table~\ref{tab:baseline} summarizes the test metrics. Across datasets, top-3 accuracy is high (0.99 or above), with solid top-1 accuracy between 0.76 and 0.83. Macro-F1 varies more widely due to class imbalance and rare activities.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Case-centric LSTM baseline -- test split metrics for next-activity prediction.}
\label{tab:baseline}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Dataset & Loss & Top-1 Acc. & Macro-F1 & Top-3 Acc. \\
\midrule
BPI 2012 & 0.5355 & 0.7569 & 0.5872 & 0.9874 \\
BPI 2017 & 0.3877 & 0.8332 & 0.5710 & 0.9906 \\
Road Traffic & 0.4833 & 0.8020 & 0.4740 & 0.9936 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Learning behavior. Validation loss curves decrease rapidly and stabilize after a few epochs for all datasets, with the validation curves closely tracking the training curves. Figure~\ref{fig:loss_curves} shows representative loss trajectories. The observed gap in BPI 2012 is small but suggests mild overfitting, which aligns with the slightly lower test macro-F1.

Error structure. Confusion matrices in Figure~\ref{fig:cms} are dominated by diagonal entries, indicating that a few frequent activities are predicted very well, but there are notable off-diagonal confusions among semantically similar or infrequent activities. This explains the difference between high top-3 accuracy and lower macro-F1.

Resource-centric evaluation. At the time of writing, the discrete-event simulator and per-resource policies are specified and implemented at the component level, but full end-to-end rollouts and metrics have not yet been executed on the benchmarks. We therefore refrain from claiming improvements over the LSTM. Our planned evaluation will use the same splits, with $N=30$ Monte Carlo rollouts per prefix and the ablation replacing learned policies by FIFO-only selection.

