Practical pitfalls. We encountered two issues relevant to reproducibility and deployment. First, generating prefixes must ensure that a next event exists; we therefore limit prefixes to length at most $T-1$ for a trace of length $T$. Second, timestamp arrays used for computing time deltas should be converted to dense NumPy arrays prior to indexing; relying on Pandas Series with non-RangeIndex can cause label-vs-position indexing errors. These details, while minor, prevent crashes and off-by-one bugs in large-scale preprocessing pipelines.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}
We presented a resource-centric blueprint for PPM that augments case-level prediction with explicit modeling of concurrency and resource contention via per-resource decision policies and a lightweight DES. Our transparent LSTM baseline achieves strong top-3 accuracy and competitive top-1 performance on standard benchmarks, establishing a reference point for the forthcoming simulator. The main expected advantages of the resource-centric approach are operational interpretability and resource-level forecasts that case-centric models cannot naturally provide. Immediate next steps are to (i) finalize end-to-end simulator rollouts over prefixes, (ii) evaluate next-activity, suffix, remaining-time, global next-event, and resource-level metrics under identical splits, and (iii) perform an ablation comparing learned per-resource policies to a FIFO-only dispatching rule. We also plan to study robustness to hidden priorities and unobserved attributes and to mitigate long-horizon drift via shorter horizons and better calibration \cite{vuksic2017utilizationod,srisurin2022evaluatingtl}. We hope this work encourages the community to integrate lightweight simulation with learned predictors for more actionable PPM.

\bibliography{references}
\bibliographystyle{agents4science_2025}

\appendix

\section*{\LARGE Supplementary Material}
\label{sec:appendix}

\section{Datasets, preprocessing, and splits}
We load XES logs using PM4Py \cite{berti2023pm4pyap}, preferring input/ and data/ folders and supporting .xes and .xes.gz. We keep case\_id, activity, lifecycle, timestamp, resource. Events are sorted by timestamp and case. For next-activity, we retain lifecycle=complete when lifecycle is present; start/complete pairs will support duration fitting for the simulator. Cases are split chronologically by start time into 70 percent training, 15 percent validation, 15 percent test. Prefixes up to length 10 are produced; time delta and since-start features are normalized using training statistics.

\section{Baseline model details}
The LSTM uses an activity vocabulary with padding index 0. Embedding size is 64; 5 continuous features are concatenated to the embedding per time step. A one-layer LSTM with hidden size 128 and dropout 0.2 encodes the sequence; the last hidden state feeds a linear classifier over activities. We train with Adam (learning rate 1e-3) for 10 epochs and select the checkpoint with best validation top-3 accuracy. We report test cross-entropy loss, top-1 accuracy, macro-F1, and top-3 accuracy.

\section{Resource-centric simulator specification}
Policies. For each resource $r$, fit a multinomial logistic model over activities using features: previous activity id for $r$ (or a special token), 4-bin time-of-day or normalized hour, per-activity queue counts and oldest-wait indicators. If $r$ has few samples, revert to a global policy trained over all resources.

Durations. For each activity, fit a log-normal to observed start-to-complete durations; if only complete is available, use a near-zero duration for feasibility in the baseline evaluation or the median when available. For sparse activities, back off to a global median.

State and events. The simulator tracks running tasks with completion times, per-activity FIFO queues of waiting cases, and resource busy/idle status. When a completion occurs, the responsible resource becomes idle; sample its next activity via $\pi_r$, pick the head of the corresponding queue, sample a duration, and schedule the completion.

Rollouts and aggregation. From a given prefix, initialize the state via replay to the current time and run $N=30$ Monte Carlo rollouts until all current cases complete or until a fixed horizon. Aggregate: next-activity distribution per ongoing case from the first step in each rollout; suffix by majority-vote decoding or n-gram aggregation; remaining time by averaging completion times; global next-event by counting which (case, activity) completes first across rollouts; resource workload as expected number of tasks in queue and busy-time ratio per resource.

\section{Evaluation plan and ablations}
Metrics include next-activity top-1 and top-3, suffix similarity via normalized Damerau--Levenshtein, remaining-time MAE, global next-event accuracy, per-resource next-task precision, and resource workload MAPE. The primary ablation replaces learned policies with FIFO-only resource selection while keeping all other components identical, isolating the effect of learning.

\section{Limitations and risks}
Hidden priorities and unobserved attributes may bias learned policies. Long-horizon rollouts can drift; smaller horizons and re-initialization from later prefixes can mitigate drift. Data sparsity at the resource level motivates hierarchical back-off to global policies. Finally, reproducibility depends on careful timestamp handling; we recommend converting timestamp series to dense arrays before positional indexing to avoid label-based indexing errors in Pandas.

\end{document}
