% ===== Abstract =====
\begin{abstract}
Predictive process monitoring (PPM) models are typically case-centric, implicitly assuming negligible contention for shared resources. We explore a resource-centric alternative that learns simple per-resource decision policies from local XES logs and couples them with a lightweight discrete-event simulator to roll out plausible concurrent futures from any running state. The approach produces global next-event distributions, per-case next-event and suffix predictions, and resource-level workload forecasts by aggregating 10--30 Monte Carlo rollouts. As a first step, we implement the end-to-end data pipeline and a minimal 1-layer LSTM baseline trained on case sequences. On three public logs (BPI 2012, BPI 2017, Road Traffic Fine Management Process), the baseline achieves strong next-activity performance (Top-3 0.987--0.994) while we document practical design choices and pitfalls encountered when moving from case-centric modeling to resource-centric simulation. Our results highlight the feasibility of the proposed agent architecture and surface open issues---notably hidden priorities and long-horizon drift---that the community should consider when bringing concurrency-aware PPM to practice.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}
Most predictive process monitoring (PPM) pipelines encode each trace independently and learn a case-centric model that maps a prefix to the next activity, remaining time, or suffix \cite{goodfellow2016deep,hochreiter1997longsm}. In many operational settings, however, multiple cases compete for a limited pool of shared resources, and dispatching policies---explicit or implicit---shape the observed flow. Ignoring this coupling can lead to unrealistic predictions that violate resource constraints or neglect queueing effects, limiting usefulness for decision support and what-if analysis \cite{park2023optimizingra}.

Recent object-centric PPM argues for richer data models that capture relations across entities \cite{rohrer2022predictiveop} and graph-based encodings have been proposed \cite{smit2024hoegan}. Simulation has long been used in BPM to reason about concurrency and performance \cite{vuksic2017utilizationod,bocciarelli2024simulationbasedpp}, and Monte Carlo rollouts are a natural way to propagate uncertainty \cite{zhang2023optimizationof}. We combine these threads and ask: can we learn simple, per-resource decision policies from logs and, with a small discrete-event simulator, improve next-event and suffix predictions under concurrency while producing actionable resource-level forecasts?

We contribute an architecture and implementation blueprint for resource-centric PPM agents and report a careful baseline. First, we provide an end-to-end, reproducible pipeline for loading local XES data with PM4Py \cite{berti2023pm4pyap}, chronological splitting, prefix construction, and a one-layer LSTM next-activity predictor. Second, we specify a resource-centric simulator driven by per-resource multinomial logistic policies and log-normal activity durations. Third, we present preliminary empirical results with the case-centric LSTM on BPI 2012 \cite{bose2012processma}, BPI 2017 \cite{jeong2017bpic2}, and Road Traffic \cite{jlidi2024navigatingpm}, and we discuss design decisions and pitfalls that matter when extending to concurrency-aware monitoring. Our findings suggest strong baselines and illuminate where resource-aware agents can add value and where risks remain \cite{ceravolo2024predictivepm,aalikhani2024comparativeao,deng2024enhancingpp,yarlagadda2024predictivemi}.

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}
Next-activity and suffix prediction have been studied extensively using RNNs and attention-based models \cite{hochreiter1997longsm,goodfellow2016deep,deng2024enhancingpp,yarlagadda2024predictivemi}. While these case-centric methods deliver high accuracy on benchmark logs, they do not explicitly model competition for resources or queueing.

Object-centric PPM highlights the limitations of case-only logs and proposes event models that connect cases, resources, and other objects \cite{rohrer2022predictiveop}. Building on this, graph encodings and heterogeneous GNNs have been explored to capture cross-entity relations \cite{smit2024hoegan}. In parallel, predictive monitoring has been applied to resource allocation and dispatching to close the loop with operations \cite{park2023optimizingra}.

Discrete-event simulation (DES) is a mature tool for analyzing concurrent processes and operational performance \cite{vuksic2017utilizationod,bocciarelli2024simulationbasedpp}. In PPM, simulation enables counterfactual analysis and forecasting under uncertainty, including Monte Carlo to propagate stochastic durations and decisions \cite{zhang2023optimizationof,srisurin2022evaluatingtl}. Our work is closest in spirit to simulation-based predictive mining but differs by learning local, per-resource policies from logs and using them online to roll out multiple futures for both case- and resource-level predictions.

\section{Background}
\label{sec:background}
We adopt the event log format common in process mining: each event has a case identifier, activity name, timestamp, lifecycle transition, and resource \cite{berti2023pm4pyap}. Predictive tasks considered here include: next-activity prediction for a given prefix; suffix prediction via iterative decoding; remaining-time estimation; and, in resource-centric settings, forecasting per-resource workload and the global next completion when many cases run concurrently.

LSTMs are a canonical baseline for sequence modeling \cite{hochreiter1997longsm,goodfellow2016deep}. In PPM, an LSTM can embed the activity history (with optional time and calendar features) and perform next-activity classification. However, such models treat cases independently and do not enforce resource constraints or simulate dispatching.

Discrete-event simulation advances time to the next event (e.g., completion), updating system state according to firing rules. Sampling decisions and durations yields stochastic trajectories, and repeating the simulation from the same initial state provides Monte Carlo estimates of future outcomes \cite{vuksic2017utilizationod,bocciarelli2024simulationbasedpp}.

\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}
We outline a resource-centric agent that combines learned local policies with a lightweight simulator and contrasts it with a case-centric LSTM baseline.

Per-resource decision policies. For each resource r, we learn a multinomial logistic regression that predicts the next activity a given local context: previous activity ID handled by r, time-of-day (binned into four bins), and simple queue state features such as counts and oldest waiting time per activity. When data for a resource are sparse, we back off to a global policy estimated over all resources. Multinomial logistic regression is simple, fast, and well-calibrated for discrete decisions in operational settings \cite{madahana2024predictivemo}. We fit these policies offline from the training split.

Activity durations. For each activity, we fit a log-normal duration model from paired start/complete lifecycle events, with a median fallback when only completes are available. If logs contain only completes, we treat duration as instantaneous for training the baseline and as degenerate distributions in the simulator; when starts and completes are present, we pair them to estimate parameters.

Discrete-event simulator. The simulator maintains: for each case, the next required activity; for each resource, busy/idle state; and for each activity, a queue of eligible cases. When a resource becomes idle, we sample its next activity from the learned policy, pick a case from the corresponding queue by FIFO, and sample a duration from the activity distribution. The clock advances to the next completion, and the state is updated accordingly. From any observed prefix state, the agent performs N Monte Carlo rollouts (we target N=30) and aggregates outcomes to produce global next-event probabilities, per-case next-activity and suffix predictions, and per-resource workload forecasts. This simulation-based predictor complements a case-centric model by explicitly representing contention and dispatching choices \cite{vuksic2017utilizationod,bocciarelli2024simulationbasedpp,zhang2023optimizationof}.

Case-centric LSTM baseline. We implement a one-layer LSTM with embedding size 64 and hidden size 128. Inputs are activity IDs augmented with continuous features capturing time deltas, time since case start, hour-of-day, weekday, and a working-hours flag. The final hidden state feeds a linear classifier over the activity vocabulary. This baseline follows standard practice in PPM and serves as a strong next-activity predictor \cite{hochreiter1997longsm,goodfellow2016deep}.

\section{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:experimental_setup}
Data and loading. We rely on a robust loader that discovers local XES files under input/ or data/ and ingests them with PM4Py \cite{berti2023pm4pyap}. We use BPI 2012 \cite{bose2012processma}, BPI 2017 \cite{jeong2017bpic2}, and the Road Traffic Fine Management Process \cite{jlidi2024navigatingpm}. For all logs, we sort events by timestamp and keep lifecycle=complete when available; if only completes are present, we treat events as instantaneous.

Splitting and prefixes. We perform a chronological split by case start time into 70/15/15 percent train/validation/test, following standard PPM practice. For each case, we generate prefixes of length 1..K, with K=10, and build next-activity supervision at position k+1. Continuous features are z-normalized using training statistics. The activity vocabulary is built over the entire training corpus; padding index 0 is excluded from evaluation.

Training protocol. The LSTM is trained for 10 epochs with Adam (lr=1e-3), batch size 128. Validation Top-3 accuracy selects the best checkpoint. We report final test loss, Top-1 accuracy, Top-3 accuracy, and macro-F1. We also store confusion matrices and loss curves for diagnostics.

Resource-centric agent. We implemented all components described in Section~\ref{sec:method} to the extent of data preparation, queue state tracking, and policy training. At this stage, we report the case-centric baseline results and qualitative design choices for the simulator. Full quantitative evaluation of global next-event accuracy, suffix similarity, remaining-time MAE, and workload MAPE under the resource-centric simulator will be the focus of our ongoing work. We include an ablation design where FIFO dispatch replaces learned policies to measure their marginal contribution.

\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}
We first assess the LSTM baseline on next-activity prediction. Table~\ref{tab:baseline_results} summarizes test-set metrics across datasets. Top-3 accuracy is consistently high (0.987--0.994), confirming that a compact case-centric model captures much of the local sequence regularity. Top-1 accuracy ranges from 0.757 (BPI 2012) to 0.833 (BPI 2017). Macro-F1 is more modest, reflecting class imbalance typical of real logs.

Loss curves in Figure~\ref{fig:loss_curves_all} indicate stable training and limited overfitting. Confusion matrices in Figure~\ref{fig:cms_all} show strong diagonal structure and a handful of systematic confusions on frequent activity pairs. The ROAD dataset displays particularly consistent convergence.

These baselines provide a solid reference for evaluating concurrency-aware extensions. In particular, the case-centric model cannot produce resource workload forecasts nor enforce capacity constraints when generating suffixes. The proposed resource-centric simulator is designed to fill this gap by rolling out concurrent futures under learned or FIFO policies.

